TI  - Hierarchical online energy management for off-road hybrid electric vehicles: integrating real-time planning with multi-agent expert knowledge
PY  - 2026
T2  - Advanced Engineering Informatics
VL  - 73
C7  - 104547
DO  - 10.1016/j.aei.2026.104547
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032183901?origin=resultslist
AB  - To address the energy management challenges of off-road hybrid electric vehicles (HEVs) in unknown driving conditions, this paper proposes a novel hierarchical online EMS underpinned by model-based reinforcement learning (MBRL). To minimize the exploration risks associated with real-world interactions, the framework integrates a vehicle state-space model with an online Markov chain model to construct an evolving virtual environment. Building upon this foundation, the lower layer employs a real-time planning (RTP) algorithm to establish a decision-planning framework that focuses on optimizing policies for instantaneous states rather than specific driving cycles, thereby ensuring adaptability across diverse scenarios. At the upper layer, an expert knowledge framework utilizing multi-agent adaptive RL algorithm is established. It dynamically calibrates the weights of mature agent strategies and performs knowledge fusion in response to real-world environmental variations, while integrating online learning to synthesize targeted guidance for the real-time decision-planning process. In comparative simulations conducted without prior knowledge of driving conditions, including off-road conditions, the proposed strategy significantly outperforms Q-learning, deep deterministic policy gradient (DDPG), model-based Q-learning (MBQL), and the equivalent consumption minimization strategy (ECMS). Specifically, it achieves results approaching the global optimum, recording fuel consumption only 2–4% higher and battery degradation less than 9% higher than the dynamic programming (DP), all while maintaining millisecond-level computation times. Furthermore, the practical applicability of the proposed method is validated through hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) testing. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
